WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.900
Dark Phoenix continues a long X-Men franchise
tradition by bringing in heroes and villains

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:10.160
familiar to the comics fans as well as referencing
classic stories.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.200
It's easy to miss them all, so here's a look
at some of the Dark Phoenix characters who

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.330
are more important than you realized.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:19.840
If you haven't seen the movie, here's your
warning that spoilers are ahead.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.380
The X-Men throw an outdoor party after they
save the endangered astronauts early on in

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:28.700
Dark Phoenix, where a young woman with long
blonde hair and striking blue eye markings

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:33.760
sings as a cloud of sparkling lights spins
in the air around and above her, choreographed

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.380
by an unseen source.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:41.240
The singer is Alison Blaire, codename Dazzler,
a mutant who can transform sound into light.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:45.840
As a member of the X-Men, she uses her powers
to blind enemies or to attack with bolts of

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:46.860
hard light.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:51.040
It's fitting Dark Phoenix is the film that
introduces her, given that the X-Men attempt

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.280
to recruit her in 1980's Uncanny X-Men #130,
just five issues before the "Dark Phoenix"

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.720
story officially kicked off.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.180
Initially named "The Disco Queen," Dazzler
would appear in Marvel Comics, and then Casablanca

00:01:04.189 --> 00:01:08.210
Records would find a singer who would take
on the persona of the hero and produce an

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.360
actual record.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.500
A lot of things went wrong, though, and Dazzler
went from being an interesting media experiment

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.820
to a D-lister mostly on the periphery of Marvel's
narrative.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.860
After Jean kills Mystique, the troubled hero
travels to a remote island.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.960
Though it's never named, it seems to be a
version of Genosha, a place that we learn

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.940
was given to Magneto and other mutants as
a homeland.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.140
"Look.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.700
I know who you are.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.159
I don't want a fight."

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:34.620
"No, you don't."

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:39.440
In the comics, Genosha begins as an island
nation ruled by humans where mutants are enslaved

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.900
by the government and their abilities are
used to serve the state.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:48.160
In the 1990 event "X-Tinction Agenda," the
X-Men, X-Factor, and the New Mutants join

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.240
forces and topple Genosha's corrupt government.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:57.900
In 1999's X-Men #87, the United Nations gives
the country to Magneto as a mutant homeland;

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:02.480
under his leadership, it grows to be something
much larger than what we see in Dark Phoenix.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.440
One of Dark Phoenix's more mysterious minor
characters is Ariki, one of the Genoshans

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.300
Magneto brings with him when he attempts to
kill Jean Grey.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:15.100
His powers revolve around his long hair, which
he uses to grapple or strike opponents.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.880
While he's initially an enemy of the X-Men,
he's arrested with the heroes and fights alongside

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.620
them in their battle against the D'Bari on
the train.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.880
Ariki doesn't appear in the comics.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.620
It was initially reported actor Andrew Stehlin
would be playing a different character.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:33.230
IGN reported that Stehlin's character was
Red Lotus, an Australian-Chinese character

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:37.140
from X-Treme X-Men whose powers have nothing
to do with hair manipulation.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.380
Not that any of it matters all that much.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.640
Ariki essentially disappears from the movie
during the train battle.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:47.180
When Professor X makes a speech after receiving
an award from the President, one of the White

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:51.660
House guests is Chris Claremont, a writer
whose work on the X-Men comics helped to make

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.520
them the household names they are now.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.700
He started writing X-Men in 1975 and wrote
them continuously until 1991.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.420
He returns to the franchise occasionally even
now.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:05.900
He wrote the first Wolverine miniseries with
artist Frank Miller, and when Logan finally

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.680
got his own ongoing series, Claremont was
its first scribe.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.000
Claremount established the foundation for
much of what we see in the films.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.400
It was Claremont who introduced the idea that
Magneto is a Holocaust survivor and that his

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.780
actions stem from his horrific experiences
during that time.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:25.580
And, of course, it was Claremont and collaborator
John Byrne who crafted "The Dark Phoenix Saga."

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.459
"We had no expectations at all when we did
the story.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.700
We just wanted to do a really great story."

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.980
There was a lot of mystery surrounding Jessica
Chastain's character leading up to the release

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.080
of Dark Phoenix.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:41.360
We now know she plays Vuk, a member of the
nearly extinct extraterrestrial race the D'Bari.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.819
She siphons some of the Phoenix power from
Jean Grey, but eventually is destroyed before

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.700
Jean takes to the stars.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:52.580
Vuk is one of Marvel's oldest supervillains,
though in the comics, she is a he.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:58.360
In his first appearance way back in 1964's
Avengers #4, Vuk turns the Avengers to stone

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:02.879
on the order of Namor the Sub-Mariner, who
promises to retrieve Vuk's crashed spaceship

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:03.700
in return.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:08.100
Little was heard from the character until
Phoenix destroyed his homeworld in 1980's

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.140
Uncanny X-Men #135.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:14.840
He's since made sporadic appearances using
the name Starhammer, and is usually intent

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.520
on getting revenge on Phoenix.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:20.680
Nightcrawler is clearly not ready for what
he experiences in Dark Phoenix.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.620
He suffers terrible guilt when he's forced
to leave Jean behind in the space shuttle.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:26.980
"Where's Jean?

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:28.080
Where is she?

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:29.720
JEAN!"

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.840
Once Jean goes bad, the blue elf is helpless
against her.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.580
Phoenix beats him up a couple of times, and
she doesn't pull her punches.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:41.840
In the battle against Magneto and his followers
in New York City, Nightcrawler is stressed

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:45.100
to his limits as the rest of the team calls
on him for aid.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:48.949
When a guard who is trying to help him is
killed by one of the D'Bari on the prison

00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:54.550
transport train, he finally snaps and we see
an uncharacteristic viciousness.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:58.949
He uses more brutal tactics than we've ever
seen from him, ultimately teleporting one

00:04:58.949 --> 00:05:03.639
of the D'Bari to the train tracks, giving
her an evil grin, and teleporting away before

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:06.060
the approaching train kills his enemy.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:10.319
He's not quite the violent, alternate-universe
version of Kurt from the comics, but he's

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.460
got that vibe.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.860
We don't see or hear much from Selene in Dark
Phoenix.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:19.200
We know she's good with knives and psychic
powers strong enough at least to hold Professor

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.240
X at bay.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.720
And in spite of her earlier mission of killing
Jean Grey, she sacrifices herself fighting

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:25.900
the D'Bari.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:29.260
Selene from the source material is much more
complex.

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:33.139
She was once known as the Black Queen of the
Hellfire Club and one of the world's most

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:37.400
powerful mutants, in part because of her wide
range of powers.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:41.820
She can suck the life force out of others
to make herself stronger, and along with the

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:46.220
telepathic powers she shows in Dark Phoenix,
she's also telekinetic.

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.100
She's also very tough to kill.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:52.120
No one knows how long she's been alive, only
that's it's been for millennia.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:57.220
More than any other X-Men film, Dark Phoenix
reveals the shameful manipulations of the

00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:59.500
seemingly benevolent Professor X.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:03.680
Not only did Xavier lie to Jean when he told
her that her father was dead, but without

00:06:03.689 --> 00:06:08.199
anyone else's knowledge, he placed psychic
blocks in her mind to protect her from traumatic

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.100
memories.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:12.660
The Phoenix Force tears those blocks down
once it inhabits Jean.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:14.820
"You think you can fix me."

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:17.600
"Jean, you are not broken."

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:22.000
Xavier being a manipulative jerk is nothing
new to the comic fans.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.280
Over the years, characters who were once almost
fanatically loyal to Xavier begin to lose

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:27.680
trust in their mentor.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:32.400
One of the more infamous examples of Professor
X's dishonesty comes during Joss Whedon's

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.280
run on Astonishing X-Men.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:38.940
When the X-Men's technologically miraculous
Danger Room comes alive and attempts to kill

00:06:38.949 --> 00:06:43.110
the team, it comes to light that Professor
X knew the Danger Room had a mind of its own

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:47.780
ever since it went online, but kept its life
a secret so no one would object to using it

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:48.600
to train.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.300
Total jerk move, Professor.

